<< lib/advantage/trading-actions-on-verb/SLBatchReturn.sleela
   SLeeLa Standard Library Definition.
   Definition: Exact aggregate return over a stated batch of N independent trades: each trade re-stakes the same capital (no compounding), so total profit is simply summed, and the batch return is reported as an exact fraction (numerator/denominator in basis points) rather than a rounded percent. >>
#sleela 3.0
material;
artifact;
pure;
gasoline;
< Answers "what would a batch of N trades return" from STATED assumptions, >
< never from a claim of confidence. Two ways to state a batch: >
<   (a) winRate/avgWin/avgLoss: an idealized batch where wins and losses are >
<       whole-number counts (winRate is in basis points of N, rounded down) >
<       and each win/loss is a percent of capital, in basis points. >
<   (b) explicit per-trade percents (up to 64 trades), for a batch built from >
<       real numbers rather than an idealized split. >
< Every trade in a batch re-stakes the SAME capital (no compounding): this >
< mirrors "2,000 independent day-trades of fixed size," not "2,000 trades of >
< a compounding account." Compounding a batch is a different, larger claim >
< (see SLLongBook/ courseware reader 06-single-trade-sensitivity.md for why >
< compounding a sustained high per-trade return is not realistic) and is >
< deliberately NOT computed here. >
< All returns are in basis points (bp; 10000 bp = 100%) so the result is an >
< EXACT fraction, not a rounded float. SLeeLa int is 64-bit and exact for >
< + - * / %, but comparisons are exact only up to 2^53 - 1, so bounds are >
< sized to keep every product under that limit. >
< The idealized-batch methods (idealBatchProfit, idealBatchReturnBp, >
< averagePerTradeBp) use an explicit validity check (idealBatchInRange / >
< the n and bp bounds in averagePerTradeBp) rather than a status code or a >
< magnitude sentinel, because a real batch can legitimately produce a loss >
< whose magnitude is as large as any sentinel this class could choose -- see >
< idealBatchInRange's comment. Callers must check validity BEFORE reading a >
< profit, the same way every sentinel-returning method here is documented. >
< addTrade (the explicit-batch path) returns a small status code instead: >
< 0 ok, 1 the 64-trade cap is full, 2 the basis-points input is out of range. >
< Illustrative only: nothing here is trading advice, and no claim is made >
< that any real trader achieves any input used here. >
class SLBatchReturn {
  < ---- Descriptive metadata (version 1.11 nested-friendly accessors) ---- >
  << Eleven catalogued attributes with getter/setter pairs. A worker-class
     lesson carries the baseline identity record. >>
  String metaVersion = "1.0.0";
  int metaId = 39340;
  String metaAuthor = "Kiro Agent";
  String metaDate = "2026-11-11";
  String metaCopyright = "SLeeLa project";
  String metaTrademark = "none";
  String metaCorporation = "none";
  < metaOrigin: National | <nation> | International | Galactic >
  String metaOrigin = "International";
  String metaBirthDate = "2026-11-11";
  < metaScienceRating: maturity/robustness on a 1..10 scale >
  int metaScienceRating = 5;
  < metaNationalSchedule: a real, citable instrument or "none" >
  String metaNationalSchedule = "none";

  String getMetaVersion() { return metaVersion; }
  void setMetaVersion(String v) { metaVersion = v; }
  int getMetaId() { return metaId; }
  void setMetaId(int v) { metaId = v; }
  String getAuthor() { return metaAuthor; }
  void setAuthor(String v) { metaAuthor = v; }
  String getDate() { return metaDate; }
  void setDate(String v) { metaDate = v; }
  String getCopyright() { return metaCopyright; }
  void setCopyright(String v) { metaCopyright = v; }
  String getTrademark() { return metaTrademark; }
  void setTrademark(String v) { metaTrademark = v; }
  String getCorporation() { return metaCorporation; }
  void setCorporation(String v) { metaCorporation = v; }
  String getOrigin() { return metaOrigin; }
  void setOrigin(String v) { metaOrigin = v; }
  String getBirthDate() { return metaBirthDate; }
  void setBirthDate(String v) { metaBirthDate = v; }
  int getScienceRating() { return metaScienceRating; }
  void setScienceRating(int v) { metaScienceRating = v; }
  String getNationalSchedule() { return metaNationalSchedule; }
  void setNationalSchedule(String v) { metaNationalSchedule = v; }

  < These three bounds are chosen TOGETHER so that EVERY product formed >
  < anywhere in this class stays at or below the exact-comparison limit >
  < 9,007,199,254,740,991 (2^53 - 1), proven once here rather than guarded >
  < ad hoc inside each method (an earlier version guarded the wrong product >
  < per call and both rejected some valid large-loss inputs AND still let a >
  < different product, in idealBatchReturnBp, silently overflow -- see use >
  < case 27's regression tests for both exact inputs that caught this): >
  <   maxTrades * maxCapital * 10000 = 8,000,000,000,000,000 (<= the limit) >
  <     -- this is the TIGHTEST bound: it is what idealBatchReturnBp needs >
  <     to convert the largest possible profit into exact basis points. >
  <   maxCapital * maxBp             =   400,000,000,000     (<= the limit) >
  <   maxTrades * maxCapital         =   800,000,000,000     (<= the limit) >
  < maxBp = 1,000,000 bp is a 100x move per trade, already far above the >
  < largest multiple used anywhere else in this folder (12x, use case 6). >
  < maxCapital is modest (400,000 cents = $4,000) because this class must >
  < support up to maxTrades trades AND exact basis-point conversion of the >
  < resulting total; a single-trade class (SLLongBook) can and does allow >
  < far larger capital because it never multiplies a profit by 10000. >
  int maxTrades = 2000000;
  int maxBp = 1000000;
  int maxCapital = 400000;

  < The reason addTrade refused, for the three codes it can return. >
  String addTradeReason(int code) {
    if (code == 0) { return "ok"; }
    if (code == 1) { return "refused: the 64-trade explicit batch is full"; }
    return "refused: a basis-points input is out of range";
  }

  < ---- Idealized batch: winRate (bp of n), winBp, lossBp as percent moves --- >
  < True exactly when every input is in range and no internal product can >
  < pass maxCapital. Callers (and idealBatchReturnBp below) check this >
  < BEFORE reading idealBatchProfit, rather than inferring validity from the >
  < size of the returned profit: a real batch of up to maxTrades trades can >
  < legitimately lose up to maxTrades * maxCapital, which is far larger in >
  < magnitude than any single sentinel value could safely sit outside of, >
  < so no magnitude-based sentinel is used for this method's result. >
  < Checks only the four INPUT bounds. No further per-call overflow guard is >
  < needed: maxTrades, maxCapital and maxBp are chosen together (see their >
  < declaration above) so every product idealBatchProfit can form is already >
  < safe for any n, capital, winBp, lossBp within these four checks -- an >
  < earlier version re-derived an overflow guard per call and got it wrong >
  < (it rejected some valid, large-loss inputs as if they were out of range; >
  < see use case 27's regression test for the exact input that caught this). >
  boolean idealBatchInRange(int n, int capital, int winRateBp, int winBp, int lossBp) {
    if (n <= 0 || n > maxTrades) { return false; }
    if (capital <= 0 || capital > maxCapital) { return false; }
    if (winRateBp < 0 || winRateBp > 10000) { return false; }
    if (winBp < 0 || winBp > maxBp || lossBp < 0 || lossBp > maxBp) { return false; }
    return true;
  }

  < returns net profit, in capital units, for an idealized batch of n trades >
  < each re-staking `capital`. winRateBp is in basis points OF n (so 10000 bp >
  < = all wins). winBp/lossBp are the per-trade percent move in basis points >
  < (lossBp is given as a POSITIVE magnitude; it is subtracted). The caller >
  < MUST check idealBatchInRange first; this method trusts that check and >
  < does not itself distinguish "rejected" from "a real, large loss" in its >
  < return value (there is no safe sentinel for that -- see above). >
  int idealBatchProfit(int n, int capital, int winRateBp, int winBp, int lossBp) {
    int wins = n * winRateBp / 10000;
    int losses = n - wins;
    int winProfit = capital * winBp / 10000;
    int lossProfit = capital * lossBp / 10000;
    return wins * winProfit - losses * lossProfit;
  }

  < The batch return, in basis points of (n * capital), exact (floor). >
  < Returns -1 only for an out-of-range input (checked explicitly via >
  < idealBatchInRange, never inferred from the profit magnitude) or a zero >
  < stake; every in-range input returns its true signed basis-point value, >
  < however large the loss. >
  int idealBatchReturnBp(int n, int capital, int winRateBp, int winBp, int lossBp) {
    if (!idealBatchInRange(n, capital, winRateBp, winBp, lossBp)) { return 0 - 1; }
    int profit = idealBatchProfit(n, capital, winRateBp, winBp, lossBp);
    int staked = n * capital;
    if (staked <= 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    if (profit >= 0) { return profit * 10000 / staked; }
    return 0 - ((0 - profit) * 10000 / staked);
  }

  < The exact per-trade AVERAGE return (in basis points) that an idealized >
  < batch implies, given its TOTAL return. This answers directly: "a batch >
  < return of X does not mean X per trade" -- it divides X by n, exactly, >
  < keeping the sign. >
  int averagePerTradeBp(int totalReturnBp, int n) {
    if (n <= 0 || n > maxTrades) { return 0 - 1 - maxBp; }
    if (totalReturnBp < 0 - maxBp || totalReturnBp > maxBp) { return 0 - 1 - maxBp; }
    if (totalReturnBp >= 0) { return totalReturnBp / n; }
    return 0 - ((0 - totalReturnBp) / n);
  }

  < ---- Explicit batch: up to 64 stated per-trade percent moves (bp), signed >
  < (a loss is a negative bp). Each trade re-stakes the same capital. ---- >
  < maxExplicitCapital is bounded so that 64 (the most trades this path can >
  < hold) * maxExplicitCapital * 10000 stays at or below the exact- >
  < comparison limit, the same product idealBatchReturnBp depends on above; >
  < this path allows a far larger per-trade capital than the idealized path >
  < because it is capped at 64 trades rather than maxTrades (2,000,000). >
  int maxExplicitCapital = 14000000000;
  int[] trades = new int[64];
  int tradeCount = 0;
  int explicitCapital = 0;
  boolean explicitBatchValid = false;

  < Result: false when capitalPerTrade is out of range (0 or above >
  < maxExplicitCapital); explicitBatchProfit/explicitBatchReturnBp return a >
  < sentinel (-1) in that case instead of computing with an unsafe capital. >
  boolean startExplicitBatch(int capitalPerTrade) {
    tradeCount = 0;
    if (capitalPerTrade <= 0 || capitalPerTrade > maxExplicitCapital) {
      explicitCapital = 0;
      explicitBatchValid = false;
      return false;
    }
    explicitCapital = capitalPerTrade;
    explicitBatchValid = true;
    return true;
  }

  < Result codes: 0 accepted, 1 full (64 already recorded), 2 bp out of range. >
  int addTrade(int signedBp) {
    if (tradeCount >= 64) { return 1; }
    if (signedBp < 0 - maxBp || signedBp > maxBp) { return 2; }
    trades[tradeCount] = signedBp;
    tradeCount = tradeCount + 1;
    return 0;
  }

  < Returns 0 (not a usable profit) when startExplicitBatch was never called >
  < successfully; callers should check explicitBatchValid or the return of >
  < startExplicitBatch before trusting this. >
  int explicitBatchProfit() {
    if (!explicitBatchValid) { return 0; }
    int sum = 0;
    int i = 0;
    while (i < tradeCount) {
      sum = sum + explicitCapital * trades[i] / 10000;
      i = i + 1;
    }
    return sum;
  }

  int explicitBatchReturnBp() {
    if (!explicitBatchValid || tradeCount == 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    int staked = tradeCount * explicitCapital;
    if (staked <= 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    int profit = explicitBatchProfit();
    if (profit >= 0) { return profit * 10000 / staked; }
    return 0 - ((0 - profit) * 10000 / staked);
  }

  int explicitTradeCount() { return tradeCount; }

  < ---- Cross-check: run the explicit batch through real SLLongBook sells, >
  < one buy+sell pair per trade (day 0 buy, day 1 sell), and compare the sum >
  < of realizedGross to explicitBatchProfit(). Returns true when they match >
  < exactly; this is the differential test the use case runs. ---- >
  < Result of crossCheckOutcome: 1 = sums matched exactly (the real check >
  < passed), 0 = sums did not match (a genuine disagreement -- this would be >
  < a bug), -1 = at least one trade could not be represented by SLLongBook at >
  < all (for example a -100% move makes the sell price 0 cents, which >
  < SLLongBook correctly refuses) so no comparison could be made. >

  < A single share at a high price keeps the cent-level bp arithmetic exact; >
  < splitting capital across many low-priced shares would floor small moves >
  < to zero cents, as it does in SLLongBook itself (integer cents). >
  int crossCheckOutcome() {
    if (tradeCount == 0 || explicitCapital <= 0) { return 0 - 1; }
    int realQty = 1;
    int buyPrice = explicitCapital;
    int actualCapital = buyPrice * realQty;
    int i = 0;
    int sumViaBook = 0;
    while (i < tradeCount) {
      SLLongBook book = new SLLongBook();
      int code1 = book.buy(0, realQty, buyPrice, 0);
      if (code1 != 0) { return 0 - 1; }
      int sellPrice = buyPrice + buyPrice * trades[i] / 10000;
      int code2 = book.sell(1, realQty, sellPrice, 0);
      if (code2 != 0) { return 0 - 1; }
      sumViaBook = sumViaBook + book.realizedGross();
      i = i + 1;
    }
    int sumDirect = 0;
    i = 0;
    while (i < tradeCount) {
      sumDirect = sumDirect + actualCapital * trades[i] / 10000;
      i = i + 1;
    }
    if (sumViaBook == sumDirect) { return 1; }
    return 0;
  }

  < Convenience wrapper: true only when the real check ran AND matched. >
  boolean crossCheckAgainstLongBook() { return crossCheckOutcome() == 1; }
}
